---
import CalorieCalculator from '@/components/calculators/CalorieCalculator';
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'What is TDEE?',
    a: 'Total Daily Energy Expenditure is everything you burn in a day: your basal metabolic rate (BMR) plus digestion, daily movement and exercise. Eat at your TDEE and your weight holds steady; eat below it and you lose.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which formula should I use?',
    a: 'Mifflin-St Jeor is the most accurate for the general population and is what the Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics recommends. Use Katch-McArdle only if you have a reliable body-fat measurement; it is better for very lean or very muscular people.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How big should my deficit be?',
    a: 'Aim for 10–25% below maintenance, which usually means losing 0.5–1% of your body weight per week. Bigger deficits cost muscle, energy and adherence. This calculator caps deficits at 25% of TDEE (or the standard 550 kcal for 0.5 kg a week, whichever is larger) and never goes below 1,200 kcal (women) or 1,500 kcal (men).',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why am I not losing weight at this number?',
    a: 'Formulas have a ±10% error and food logging is usually 20–30% under. Track your weekly average weight for three weeks. If it has not moved, cut 100–150 kcal or add 2,000 steps, then re-check. See our <a href="/guides/weight-loss-plateaus/">plateau guide</a>.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should I eat back exercise calories?',
    a: 'If you chose an activity level that already includes your workouts, no. Fitness trackers overestimate exercise burn by 30–90%, so eating those calories back usually erases the deficit.',
  },
];
---

<ToolLayout
  slug="calories"
  title="Calorie Calculator"
  description="Free TDEE and calorie-deficit calculator. Get your maintenance calories and a safe daily target for fat loss using Mifflin-St Jeor, Harris-Benedict or Katch-McArdle."
  lede="Find your maintenance calories and a daily target that loses fat at a pace you can sustain. Built on the equations dietitians actually use, with guard rails so it never suggests something reckless."
  faq={faq}
>
  <CalorieCalculator client:load slot="calculator" />

  <h2>How this calculator works</h2>
  <p>
    Weight change is governed by energy balance. Your body burns a baseline amount of energy at rest
    (BMR), which is multiplied by an activity factor to estimate your Total Daily Energy Expenditure
    (TDEE). Eating below TDEE creates a deficit; roughly 7,700 kcal of cumulative deficit equals one
    kilogram of body weight.
  </p>
  <p>
    We estimate BMR with the <strong>Mifflin-St Jeor</strong> equation by default, because
    validation studies consistently show it predicts measured metabolic rate within 10% for more
    people than any other formula. Harris-Benedict is offered for comparison. Katch-McArdle uses
    lean mass instead of total weight, which matters if you are unusually lean or muscular.
  </p>
  <h2>Choosing a rate of loss</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>0.25–0.5 kg per week</strong> (0.5–1 lb): gentle, easy to sustain, almost no muscle
      loss. Ideal if you have less than 10 kg to lose or you lift weights.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>0.5–0.75 kg per week</strong>: the standard recommendation for most people.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>1 kg per week or more</strong>: only realistic with a lot of weight to lose and high
      maintenance calories. The calculator will cap the deficit if it exceeds 25% of your TDEE.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <h2>What to do with the number</h2>
  <p>
    Eat at your target for two to three weeks and weigh yourself daily, comparing weekly averages.
    If you are losing at roughly the expected pace, change nothing. If not, adjust by 100–150 kcal
    and repeat. Your target will need to drop slowly as you get lighter; the{' '}
    <a href="/tools/timeline/">timeline tool</a> shows exactly how much.
  </p>
</ToolLayout>
